Task: Redesign ONLY the BlueMind iOS camera experience to feel modern, native, and integrated into the app, inspired by the attached reference camera UI.

The current camera works, but I want the camera experience to feel much more polished and current.

Do NOT copy another app pixel-for-pixel.

Use the reference only for interaction style and layout inspiration, then create a BlueMind-native version using our existing design language and iOS system materials.

⸻

GOAL

The camera should feel like it belongs inside BlueMind.

I want:

* fast opening;
* large live preview;
* simple controls;
* native iOS glass/material;
* minimal dark UI;
* immediate captured-photo preview;
* clean Retake / Use Photo flow;
* no unnecessary clutter.

⸻

CAMERA OPENING

When the user taps Camera from the attachment menu:

* dismiss the attachment menu immediately;
* start presenting the camera at the same time;
* avoid a visible dead pause;
* camera should feel almost instant.

Do not wait for unnecessary UI animations or processing before showing the camera.

⸻

LIVE CAMERA LAYOUT

Use a clean fullscreen/near-fullscreen native iOS camera presentation.

Main layout:

* black background;
* large live camera preview;
* preserve correct camera aspect ratio;
* no stretched preview;
* respect safe areas.

Bottom controls:

* large circular shutter button centered;
* Back/Close button on the left;
* camera-switch button on the right.

Top controls:

keep only genuinely useful controls, such as flash if already supported.

Do not overload the screen.

⸻

NATIVE IOS STYLE

Use real native iOS material/glass controls where possible.

Buttons should feel like current iOS:

* circular;
* translucent;
* subtle blur;
* system-style iconography;
* large comfortable touch targets.

Avoid fake glass made only from dark transparent circles.

⸻

BLUEMIND BRANDING

Keep BlueMind branding subtle.

Use the existing official primary color:

BLUE_MIND_COLORS.primary

/ --bm-primary

current value:

#193B68

Use it only where appropriate for active/accent states.

Do not turn the entire camera blue.

⸻

AFTER CAPTURE

Immediately after taking a photo:

show the captured image in a clean preview screen.

The user should clearly have:

Retake

and

Use Photo

Keep the layout minimal.

Do not make the user wait for upload/backend processing before seeing the captured image.

⸻

USE PHOTO FLOW

When the user taps Use Photo:

* return to the chat composer immediately;
* show ONE attachment for that photo;
* show a fast local thumbnail immediately;
* preserve the same stable attachment ID;
* continue any high-resolution/background processing asynchronously;
* when original/full-resolution data is ready, update that same attachment.

Never create a duplicate attachment.

⸻

RETAKE FLOW

When the user taps Retake:

* discard only the current temporary capture;
* return immediately to live camera;
* do not add anything to composer;
* do not create stale attachment state.

⸻

IMAGE QUALITY

Preserve the original captured image at full available resolution.

The camera preview/composer thumbnail may use a lightweight representation for speed, but the real attachment and fullscreen viewer must retain the original/highest-quality image.

Do not permanently compress the captured image just to make the UI faster.

⸻

PERFORMANCE

Avoid heavy synchronous processing on the main thread.

Do not block camera dismissal or composer return while:

* compressing;
* generating thumbnails;
* preparing upload data;
* reading metadata.

The UI should respond first, background work second.

⸻

DO NOT CHANGE

Do not modify:

* Photos picker flow;
* Web camera behavior;
* backend upload API;
* chat logic;
* transcription;
* unrelated composer design.

This task is only the iOS camera experience and capture flow.

⸻

REQUIRED TESTS

Test on a real iPhone:

1. Open attachment menu.
2. Tap Camera.
3. Verify camera appears with no noticeable pause.
4. Capture a photo.
5. Verify preview appears immediately.
6. Tap Retake and verify no attachment is created.
7. Capture again.
8. Tap Use Photo.
9. Verify composer returns immediately.
10. Verify exactly one attachment appears.
11. Verify no duplicate appears after processing completes.
12. Verify original/high-resolution image is preserved.
13. Verify camera-switch button works.
14. Verify close/back works correctly.

⸻

FINAL RESULT

I want the camera to feel:

fast + native + minimal + modern + BlueMind

rather than a generic camera screen bolted onto the app.

After implementation, report:

1. Which iOS camera component/API is used.
2. Which files changed.
3. How camera presentation was made faster.
4. How Retake / Use Photo are implemented.
5. How duplicate attachments are prevented.
6. How original image quality is preserved.
